---

<Layout title="Notes | Standard Atmosphere Calculator">
	<Breadcrumb crumbs={[
		{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
		{ label: 'Online tools', href: '/online-tools' },
		{ label: 'Standard Atmosphere Calculator' },
	]} />
	<h1>Standard Atmosphere Calculator</h1>
	<AtmosphereNav active="notes" />
	<h2>About the U.S. Standard Atmosphere</h2>
	<p>
	The <a href="https://www.pdas.com/atmosdownload.html" target="_blank">U.S. Standard Atmosphere</a> (USSA) is a model of Earth's atmospheric
	temperature, pressure and other properties from -5 to 1,000 km (-16,404 to 3,280,840 ft), an extension of the International Standard Atmosphere 
	(ISA). Properties are calculated for dry air; humid air can be up to 2% less dense than dry air at the same temperature and pressure. The USSA is 
	most representative of mid-latitude conditions.
	</p>
	<h2>Standard sea-level conditions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Pressure: 1 atm (1,013.25 hPa / 760 mmHg / 14.6959 psi / 29.9213 inHg)</li>
		<li>Temperature: 15°C (59°F / 288.15 K / 518.67°R)</li>
		<li>Lapse rate: -6.5°C/km (-0.00357°F/ft) under 11 km geopotential, varies above 11 km</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
	Note that adjusting sea-level temperature or air temperature in this calculator only affects temperatures below the -56.5°C (-69.7°F) height in
	the troposphere/lower stratosphere, which may be up to 20 km. Above that, temperatures are fixed at USSA values. This is because temperatures 
	in the stratosphere and above aren't strongly affected by surface conditions.
	</p>
	<h2>Changes with altitude</h2>
	<p>
	In the troposphere (below 11 km), temperature decreases with altitude at the standard lapse rate. In the stratosphere (11-47 km), temperature is 
	initially constant before increasing with altitude due to UV absorption by ozone. Temperature decreases again in the mesosphere (47-86 km) and 
	rises in the thermosphere (above 86 km). Pressure decreases roughly exponentially throughout the atmosphere.
	</p>
	<p>
	Pressure can be adjusted to sea level in two ways: <b>sea-level pressure</b> and <b>altimeter setting</b>. Sea-level pressure extrapolates the
	measured pressure to sea level using the measured temperature, altitude, an assumed lapse rate, and the ideal gas law. Altimeter setting instead
	finds the standard pressure ratio between the given altitude and sea level, then applies that ratio to the measured pressure.
	</p>
	<p>
	Example: In Denver (elevation 1,656 m / 5,433 ft), a summer day might be 30°C (86°F) with a pressure of 833 hPa. Extrapolating these conditions
	down to sea level using the standard lapse rate gives 40.761°C (105.370°F) and <b>1,000.62 hPa</b>. The USSA pressure ratio at 1,656 m is 0.818689, 
	so the altimeter setting is 833 / 0.818689 = <b>1,017.48 hPa</b> (30.0462 inHg). At these conditions, the pressure altitude is 1,622 m, slightly 
	below the actual elevation because the pressure is slightly above standard. Due to the high temperature, however, the density altitude is much higher 
	at 2,497 m.
	</p>
	<h2>Geometric versus geopotential altitude</h2>
	<p>
	Geometric altitude is the actual height above mean sea level, while geopotential altitude is a "corrected" altitude representing the altitude in a
	uniform gravitational field of 9.80665 m/s<sup>2</sup> that would require the same work to reach as the actual altitude in the real gravity field. 
	Gravity weakens slightly at higher altitudes, due to the greater distance from earth's center, so at very high altitudes geopotential altitude is 
	significantly less than geometric altitude.
	</p>
